#doc (Manual) "Verification" =>

%%%
tag := "verification"
%%%

This chapter describes how the main theorem can be checked independently of this text and of the
library's own definitions. A short Lean file, the challenge, states the theorem using Mathlib alone.
A second file, the solution, proves the same statement from the library. Lake Comparator checks
that the two statements match and replays the solution with the Lean kernel, allowing only the
axioms `propext`, `Quot.sound` and `Classical.choice`.

The challenge defines the two permutations of the complex plane directly from their disk
actions. It asks one question using only these definitions, the radical
$`\sqrt{3+(1+\sqrt5)/2}`, and Mathlib. The solution identifies that group with the formal
development and proves the statement. Open the Lean panel to compare the precise statement with
the {bpref "critical_radius_five"}[main theorem].

:::theorem "challenge_finite" (lean := "FiveDisk.finite_iff") (uses := "critical_radius_five, generated_by_two")
For every real $`r`, the group generated by the clockwise fifth-turns of the closed disks of
radius $`r` about $`-1` and $`1` is finite if and only if $`r<\sqrt{3+(1+\sqrt5)/2}`.
:::

:::proof "challenge_finite"
Both groups are generated by the same two permutations, so the challenge's group at radius
$`r` is $`GG_5(r)`. The statement is then the equivalence of the
{bpref "critical_radius_five"}[main theorem].
:::

This is the sharp form of the result: the group is finite below the threshold and infinite at
the threshold and above it. The negative check `scripts/check-comparator-negative.sh` makes two
mutations of the challenge, and Comparator must reject each: replacing $`<` by $`\le`, and
changing the definition of the radius.

*Running the check*

From a checkout with [elan](https://github.com/leanprover/elan) and Linux bubblewrap with user
namespaces available, run

```
bash scripts/get-mathlib-cache.sh
lake build
lake comparator --config Comparator/FiveDisk/comparator.json
bash scripts/check-comparator-negative.sh
```

The first command fetches the imported Mathlib modules and their dependencies, and the second
builds the library. The third runs Comparator on the challenge and the solution. The last makes
the two mutations, each in its own disposable copy, and succeeds only when Comparator rejects
both, the first as a mismatched statement and the second as a mismatched definition.

*Independent replay*

The published site is built and checked by GitHub Actions, which runs Comparator on Linux and
publishes the site only if the check succeeds. Each published version carries a verification
record, a file published beside the [site home](/index.html) that names the commit, the Actions run, the
toolchain, the kernel checks and the theorem names; the line below this paragraph reports it, with
links to the commit and to the run. Every run, with its full log, is listed in the repository's
[Actions history](https://github.com/eric-vergo/TDCSG/actions). A copy of the site built on
another computer has no verification record, and the line below says so. The Project Stats page
lists what the blueprint found in the compiled declarations.

Read the {repoFile "Comparator/FiveDisk/Challenge.lean"}[standalone challenge],
{repoFile "Comparator/FiveDisk/Solution.lean"}[solution], and
{repoFile "Comparator/FiveDisk/comparator.json"}[allowed axioms] at the commit this site was built from. The challenge
compares formal statements. Checking how those statements express the intended mathematical
problem also requires reading their definitions.

